  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Geometry of higher helicities — We revisit an interpretation of higher-dimensional helicities and Hopf–Novikov invariants from the point of view of the Brownian ergodic theorem.\n\n**Geometry of higher helicities**\n\n*BORIS A. KHESIN*\n\n23 pp, 16 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We revisit an interpretation of higher-dimensional helicities and Hopf–Novikov invariants from the point of view of the Brownian ergodic theorem. We also survey various results related to Arnold’s the- orem on the asymptotic Hopf invariant on three-dimensional manifolds and recent work on linking of a vector ﬁeld with a foliation, the asymp- totic crossing number, short path systems, and relations with the Calabi invariant. 2000 Math. Subj. Class. 37A15, 55Q25, 76W05.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 2 What is Helicity?; 3 Three-Dimensional Ramifications; 3.1 Bilinear and multi-linear forms.; 3.2 Energy estimates.; 3.3 Ergodic meaning of the Calabi invariant.; 3.4 Short paths systems.; 4 Linking of a Vector Field with a Measured Fo; 4.1 Linking of a curve with a foliation; 4.2 Linking of a vector field with a foliation; 4.3 Linking of several measured foliations.; 5 Linking of Foliations and Brownian Motion; 6 Novikov's Integrals; Acknowledgments; References",
      "lang": "en",
